    "final_text": "I've built the todo app with all four commands, and all 88 tests pass under `npm test`. There are no dependencies: it uses only Node's built-in modules and test runner, and needs Node 18 or later.\n\n**Files**\n- `index.js`: the command-line entry point. It reads the command, runs it, prints the result and sets the exit code.\n- `lib/todos.js`: the core logic for loading and saving `todos.json`, adding, finding and deleting todos, checking IDs, and formatting the list.\n- `test/todos.test.js`: unit tests for the core logic.\n- `test/cli.test.js`: end-to-end tests that run `node index.js` as a separate process, each in a fresh temporary folder.\n- `package.json` (with `\"test\": \"node --test\"`), a `README.md`, and `todos.json` added to `.gitignore`.\n\n**Example session**\n```\n$ node index.js add \"Buy groceries\"   \u2192 Added todo #1: Buy groceries\n$ node index.js complete 1            \u2192 Completed todo #1: Buy groceries\n$ node index.js list                  \u2192 1. [x] Buy groceries\n$ node index.js delete 7              \u2192 Error: No todo found with ID 7.   (exit code 1)\n```\n\n**Edge cases handled (all tested)**\n- **Missing arguments:** a missing text or ID gives an error that shows the correct usage.\n- **Invalid IDs:** `abc`, `0`, `-1`, `1.5` and `1e2` are all rejected.\n- **Unknown IDs and extra arguments:** an ID that doesn't exist, or more arguments than a command takes, gives an error.\n- **Empty text:** empty or whitespace-only todo text is rejected.\n- **Empty list:** `list` prints \"No todos yet\u2026\" instead of nothing, whether `todos.json` is missing, empty, or `[]`.\n- **Unknown command or no command:** prints the usage text with exit code 1.\n- **Damaged `todos.json`:** if the file is broken or has unexpected contents, every command stops with a clear error and leaves the file untouched rather than overwriting it.\n\n**Choices I made where the spec was open**\n- **ID numbering:** a new todo's ID is one more than the highest existing ID, and `todos.json` stays a plain array as specified. The downside is that if you delete the newest todo, its ID gets reused by the next one. Never reusing IDs would mean storing a counter, which changes the file format, so I didn't do it.\n- **Unquoted text works:** `add Walk the dog` adds \"Walk the dog\". Leading and trailing spaces are trimmed.\n- **Completing a done todo is not an error:** it prints a note and exits with code 0.\n- **Safe saves:** the app writes to a temporary file and then swaps it in, so an interrupted save can't leave `todos.json` half-written.\n\nAs a check that the tests actually catch mistakes, I temporarily changed `[x]` to `[X]` in the code: 3 tests failed, and I then restored the original. I haven't committed anything; the new files are in the working tree for you to review.",
